NPC: net_seller
LOCATION: waterfront
SCHEDULE: morning=waterfront_net_loft, midday=waterfront_net_loft, evening=closed, night=closed

TOPIC: default
  GATE: coat = plain AND visit_count(net_seller) >= 2
  TAG: net_seller_plain_repeat
  TIME: 0
  NET SELLER: "Still walking the timbers without the brass, Officer?\nMind the tar bucket by the bench."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: always
  TAG: net_seller_default
  TIME: 2
  NET SELLER: "Nets, lines, or crabbing pots. Otherwise you're in my light."

TOPIC: crew_omission
  GATE: topic_done(local_historian, crew_omission_official) AND NOT topic_done(net_seller, crew_omission)
  TAG: net_seller_crew_omission
  LABEL: "Ask why the town's history leaves out the crew's names"
  TIME: 4
  WALTER CORWIN: "The men who drowned in 1823 — their names aren't in any record."
  NET SELLER: "Ours are."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Where?"
  NET SELLER: "In the families. In the boats we named after them.\nIn the berths we don't give to strangers."
  [She does not look up from the netting needle.]
  NET SELLER: "You're asking why the town didn't write them down.\nThe town didn't lose them.\nWe did."
  NOTEBOOK: net_seller_crew_omission | "The net seller said the drowned crew's names are preserved in the harbor families, not in civic records. She drew a clear line between 'the town' and 'we.'"

TOPIC: harbor_currents
  GATE: evidence(sub_harbor_drag) AND NOT topic_done(net_seller, harbor_currents)
  TAG: net_seller_currents
  LABEL: "Ask about the unnatural stillness before the murders"
  TIME: 4
  WALTER CORWIN: "Mrs. Peake said the tide went dead flat at three in the morning, three nights back."
  NET SELLER: "Lydia Peake hears with her ears. I watch the lead weights."
  [She holds up a hemp line strung with grey lead sinkers.]
  NET SELLER: "A flat surface is a trick for men in rowboats, Officer.\nDown twelve fathoms, the water was running inland. Not toward the bay. Toward the hill."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Toward the hill?"
  NET SELLER: "Toward the bluff where the estates sit. The line didn't drift east on the ebb.\nIt pulled straight into the rock beneath the kitchen wing."
  NOTEBOOK: net_seller_currents_1 | "Net seller observed deep harbor currents running unnaturally inland toward the estate bluff rather than out with the ebb tide."
  EVIDENCE: inland_subsea_current

TOPIC: stone_sinkers
  GATE: NOT topic_done(net_seller, stone_sinkers)
  TAG: net_seller_sinkers
  LABEL: "Ask why the skiffs by the slipway use grooved stones instead of iron anchors"
  TIME: 4
  WALTER CORWIN: "I noticed three dories tied at the slip with grooved granite stones for anchors. Iron's cheap enough at the chandlery."
  NET SELLER: "Iron rings."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Rings?"
  NET SELLER: "Drop a five-prong iron grapnel on the ledge out past the point at three in the morning, and the clatter travels three miles through the granite shelf like a tapped telegraph line.\nGranite against granite makes a thud. Nothing wakes up.\nDown here, we like what's quiet."
  NOTEBOOK: net_seller_stone_sinkers | "Net seller explained the dories use grooved granite stones instead of iron grapnels because iron striking the undersea granite shelf rings like a tapped telegraph wire across the bay."
  EVIDENCE: stone_sinker_discipline

TOPIC: copper_rings
  GATE: coat = plain AND evidence(observer_color_tell) AND NOT topic_done(net_seller, copper_rings)
  TAG: net_seller_rings
  LABEL: "Ask why the harbor people wear beaten copper rings"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "The mason wears a band made from an anchor link. Is that common here?"
  NET SELLER: "You'll have to ask him about his belongings."
  [She lifts a snagged loop clear of the needle. Her thumb rests briefly against the worn metal of her gauge.]
  WALTER CORWIN: "I thought you might know him."
  NET SELLER: "I know this net wants finishing."
  NOTEBOOK: net_seller_ring_discipline | "The net seller declined to discuss the mason's band and returned to repairing her net. She offered no explanation for it."
  # Historical evidence ID retained; this records a refusal, not an explained technique.
  EVIDENCE: eye_anchoring_discipline
